You must convert a `std::weak_ptr` to a `std::shared_ptr` using its `lock()` method. `lock()` returns a `std::shared_ptr` if the object still exists, or an empty `std::shared_ptr` (i.e., `nullptr`) if the object has been deallocated. You should always check the returned `shared_ptr` for validity before dereferencing it.
```cpp
std::weak_ptr<int> wp;
// ... later ...
if (std::shared_ptr<int> sp = wp.lock()) {
    // Object is still alive, safely use *sp
    std::cout << *sp << std::endl;
} else {
    // Object has been deallocated
    std::cout << "Object expired." << std::endl;
}
```

The class `Foo` must publicly inherit from `std::enable_shared_from_this<Foo>`. Then, within a member function, `shared_from_this()` can be called to obtain a valid `std::shared_ptr` to the current instance. This ensures that the returned `shared_ptr` shares ownership with existing `shared_ptr`s, rather than creating a new, independent `shared_ptr` with a separate control block.
```cpp
class Foo : public std::enable_shared_from_this<Foo> {
public:
    std::shared_ptr<Foo> get_shared_ptr() {
        return shared_from_this();
    }
};
```
